`showMainWindow()` only calls `show()` and `focus()`. The card is a non-activating panel, so clicking a chip leaves Kaipu inactive, and the repo has already found that this is not enough: `recording-hub.test.ts:728` says *"Showing the window alone leaves it unfocused behind the other app"* and expects `app.focus({ steal: true })`. When the user is in another app (the case this window is for), step 5 of the test plan ("Kaipu comes to the front") will probably fail. Prepare or the editor would open behind the other app. `bringAppToFront()` in this file already does `showMainWindow()`, `applyDockPolicy()` and `app.focus({ steal: true })`.

```suggestion
    openInMain: (handOff) => {
      bringAppToFront();
      sendToRenderer(IPC_CHANNELS.recordingReadyOpen, handOff);
    },
```

The ready card's Drag calls `markCopySeen(recording.assetId)` before `startFileDrag` (`export-status-cards.tsx:234`), and `ready-copies.ts` says a copy stays unseen until the user opens it or drags it out. This new Drag handle skips that step. So a copy dragged straight from the master's page keeps its outline and the "copy ready" chip on the Library page.

```suggestion
                    markCopySeen(copy.assetId);
                    window.electronAPI.startFileDrag(copy.id!, copy.title);
```

`.body` can shrink to zero (`min-height: 0`), but its content cannot: the closed groups are `flex: none`, `.groupWhat` is `flex: none`, and `.rows` keeps a 72px floor. Take a window near the 560px minimum (`BASE_MIN_HEIGHT`) with all four groups present. By my estimate (not checked in a browser), the fixed heights come to roughly 560px, while the dialog gets about 510px. `.body` keeps overflow visible, so the list draws over the summary line and the Fix button instead of being contained. Scrolling `.body` as a fallback keeps the footer clear. When everything fits, nothing scrolls there, so the one-scroll design is unchanged.

```suggestion
.body {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 0;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: var(--space-sm);
  /* Fallback for very short windows: the footer stays clear when even the minimum list does not fit. */
  overflow-y: auto;
}
```

This only checks the folder's color, but the doc comment above promises that "a name or color the user already changed, here or on another Mac, is left as it is". Take a Default folder (for example on Drive) renamed to "Studio" on another Mac and still `rose`, with this Mac's registry holding a stale "Default workspace" cache. The upgrade sets `pendingIdentity`, and `availability()` then writes `name: "Default workspace"` and `initials: "DW"` over the folder's "Studio". Comparing the name as well closes this.

```suggestion
      if (
        file.kind === "ok" &&
        (file.identity.color !== "rose" || file.identity.name !== DEFAULT_WORKSPACE_NAME)
      ) {
        upgrade = false;
      }
```
